  {
   "id": 1,
   "type": "note",
   "x": 40,
   "y": 20,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "WELCOME. You are looking at a living spiking neural network: rhythmic bursts from a pacemaker cluster travel across a sheet of 15,000 neurons as waves.\n\nDrag the 3D view to orbit, scroll to zoom, click any neuron to see its connections and voltage. This graph below builds everything. Single click selects a node; double click opens its settings. The DOCS button (top right) explains every tool.",
    "width": 300
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": 2,
   "type": "note",
   "x": 40,
   "y": 220,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "Networks flow top to bottom: shape nodes define volumes (in micrometers), scatter fills them with neurons, merge combines groups, connect grows the synapses, stimulus injects current, output runs the simulation.",
    "width": 300
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": 3,
   "type": "note",
   "x": 40,
   "y": 360,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "TRY THIS: double click the sphere feeding the pacemaker scatter (orange node to the right), then drag the arrows that appear in the 3D view. The wave source moves with it, live.\n\nThen try the RES slider in the top bar: the network rebuilds smaller but keeps the same behavior.",
    "width": 300
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
